  set:  # 185d
    fields:  # 185d
      root_cause: "CORRECTED 2026-08-23 after reading the producer and measuring. The named mechanism (module_attr_ref's membership in _CALL_SITE_EDGE_TYPES) is a CONSUMER symptom, not the root cause, and removing that membership is the worse of the two fixes: measured on hypergumbo's own tree it deletes 6 genuine third-party attribute-read disclosures (google, mypy, navigator, sentence_transformers, tokenizers, builtins) to remove 2 artifacts (std, std.cmp). ROOT CAUSE: analyze/base.py emit_module_attribute_refs with scoped_path=True treats EVERY scoped_identifier outside callee position as an attribute read, so a `use` path and a return-type path are both emitted as attribute reads. The `use` case is a literal duplicate home -- the same statement is already emitted as an `imports` edge in the same run, in the '::' spelling -- so the fact the gate deliberately excludes as `imports` re-enters as module_attr_ref. Present in both scoped_path languages (rust `use`/return-type, cpp `using`/return-type); absent in python, whose own helper emits neither for `import os` nor for `p: os.PathLike`. Fix belongs in the shared emitter (one home, both languages) via a per-language skip-context tuple. TWO further causes exist and are filed separately, not folded here: rust.yaml declares zero module_completeness entries so module_io_is_enumerated is False for every rust module in BOTH spellings; and the left-recursive prefix walk emits one edge per nesting depth, of which at most one can match a catalogue row."  # 185d
- op: discuss  # f98b
  at: '2026-08-24T03:35:41Z'  # f98b
  by: agent  # f98b
  actor: jgstern_agent  # f98b
  clock: 4  # f98b
  nonce: f98b  # f98b
  message: "FIX BUILT, MEASURED, AND DELIBERATELY NOT MERGED. Branch\njgstern-agent/fix/scoped-attr-ref-import-and-type-positions carries the\nproducer fix and 13 tests, all green, ratchet 670 delta +0. It is correct and\nit must not ship yet.\n\nWHAT THE FIX IS. emit_module_attribute_refs gains a per-language\n`skip_context_kinds` tuple beside the node_kinds / object_field_names tuples it\nalready takes; a candidate's first ancestor OUTSIDE node_kinds says what the\npath is doing (`use_declaration` / `using_declaration` = import,\n`scoped_type_identifier` / `generic_type` / `function_definition` /\n`declaration` = type position) and those are skipped. One home, both\nscoped_path languages. Positive controls in the same test class: the genuine\nreads `std::env::consts::OS` and `std::cout` still emit, a read in the BODY of\na function whose RETURN TYPE is skipped still emits, and javascript (which\npasses no tuple) emits exactly what it did before.\n\nLAYER 1 (unit) and LAYER 2 (analyzer) green: `use std::fs;` now emits zero\nmodule_attr_ref edges and still emits its `imports` edge\n(rust:std::fs:0-0:module:module), so the fact keeps exactly one home.\n\nLAYER 3 (published output) IS WHY THIS IS NOT MERGED. On THIS ITEM'S OWN FILED\nFIXTURE:\n\n  before:  ? net_send inconclusive (std, std.io, std.net)\n           ? fs_write inconclusive (same three)\n           x fs_read  violated, 1 chain          <- control\n  after:   v net_send CONFIRMED  \"No net_send chains found.\"\n           v fs_write CONFIRMED  \"No fs_write chains found.\"\n           x fs_read  violated, 1 chain          <- control still fires\n\nThe crate calls `sock.send(payload)` on a `&UdpSocket` and `w.write_all(x)` on\na `W: Write`. Both are catalogued rust.yaml sinks. The fix removes the gate\nthat was withholding these verdicts for an unrelated reason and there is\nnothing underneath it.\n\nSO THIS ITEM'S OWN SEVERITY ASSESSMENT IS REFUTED. The description says \"The\ndirection is fail-safe (inconclusive, not confirmed), so this is a PRECISION\ndefect in the gate, not a false-confirm.\" That is false. The inconclusive was\nload-bearing safety arrived at BY ACCIDENT, and removing the accident exposes\ntwo false confirms of exactly the INV-fibis / INV-nuhun class -- on a function\nnamed `exfiltrate`.\n\nBLOCKED BY INV-polad, filed with the mechanism: a 77-name method denylist in\nrust.py overlaps rust.yaml's declared sinks on ten names (UdpSocket.send and\nio::Write.write among them) so `s.send(b)` emits no edge at all, AND the rust\nanalyzer never stamps meta.call_construct, so INV-fibis's untyped-receiver\ncaveat -- the channel designed to catch precisely this -- cannot fire for rust\nat all. Both hold today; either alone would have left a caveat behind.\n\nLIVE.md rule 18 is the lesson, in the opposite direction from how it is\nwritten there: a gate that looks like the bug WAS partly the fix, and the\nright move was to measure the published output of my own shipped diff before\nmerging it, not after.\n\nNOT MEASURED: whether the same unmasking happens for cpp (the fix touches both\nlanguages and only rust was run end-to-end); how many real Rust repos would\nmove from inconclusive to a bare confirm once this lands."  # f98b
